{
  "program": "J",
  "title": "Tied stopwords break substitution invariance of the directional-pair test",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": false,
  "statement": "In one constructed 5,000-token book with 20 equally frequent types, each of 20 cyclic bijective relabellings yields exactly the same type counts and ordering structure. The lexicographic tie-break changes which original type is discarded and changes one-sided shuffle p values from .01 to .55 (compress) and .01 to .54 (barrier), crossing .05. Choosing the first occurring tied type keeps the discarded original type, score and seeded p value unchanged under all 20 relabellings. This demonstrates a tie-breaking artifact, not a calibrated alternative.",
  "check": "For real and synthetic inputs with ties and no ties, compare substitution-invariant tie rules and explicitly valid conditional randomization schemes. In particular, a first-occurrence selection depends on order: either recompute the complete selection statistic in each null draw or derive the appropriate conditional test. Evaluate type-I error and power on multiple independent documents and structured non-language controls before manuscript use.",
  "method": "Construct a deliberately heterogeneous book: 250 alternations of w00,w01 followed by 4,500 tokens comprising 250 each of w02\u2013w19 shuffled with seed 731. Every type occurs exactly 250 times. Cut into consecutive 80-token blocks, last 40. Apply all 20 cyclic label permutations, each a bijection of whole tokens, and remove one most frequent type using either lexicographic label or first appearance. For each 99 within-block shuffles from seed 921, keep the chosen removed type fixed and use the P-J-039 directional imbalance threshold >5 and add-one upper-tail p value. Compression and barrier policies are measured separately. All permutations share the same text and shuffle seeds, so the 20 outputs are transformations of one example, not independent trials.",
  "limitations": "This deliberately artificial book was constructed to make tied types have different local order. The fixed-stopword shuffle for the first-appearance rule is a diagnostic, not generally exchangeable under the null because the choice itself uses order. A change in p does not show a difference in underlying language or evidence of a cipher. No historical or manuscript data are used.",
